<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Adapted from FOPP "Flow of Execution of the for Loop". GNU FDL 1.3; see for-loops/ch-loops.ptx. -->
<section xml:id="sec-flow-of-execution">
  <title>Flow of Execution of the <c>for</c> Loop</title>
  <introduction>

    <p>
      As a program runs, the computer always keeps track of which line it will run next.
      We call this the <term>flow of execution</term>, or <term>control flow</term>.
      You can think of it as the computer pointing its finger at one line at a time.
    </p>

    <p>
      Until now, control flow has gone from top to bottom, one line at a time.
      We call that <term>sequential</term> flow.
      An <c>if</c> statement changed that a little: it can skip a code block.
      A loop changes it even more: it can jump <em>back up</em> and run a code block again.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here are the exact steps a <c>for</c> loop follows.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li><p>Run the <term>start</term>. (This happens once.)</p></li>
      <li><p>Check the <term>condition</term>. If it is <c>false</c>, the loop is over: skip to the first line after the closing <c>}</c>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Run the <term>body</term>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Run the <term>update</term>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Go back to step 2.</p></li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Notice that the condition is always checked <em>before</em> the body runs.
      That means the very last thing a loop does is check the condition and find it <c>false</c>.
      The condition that ends the loop is called the <term>terminating condition</term>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-tracing">
    <title>Tracing a Loop</title>

    <p>
      To <term>trace</term> a loop means to follow these steps by hand and write down what happens.
      A table makes it easy.
      Let's trace this loop:
    </p>

    <program language="javascript">
      <code>
for (let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}
console.log("done");
      </code>
    </program>

    <table xml:id="table-trace-1">
      <title>Tracing a loop that counts 0 to 2</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Step</cell>
          <cell>Value of <c>i</c></cell>
          <cell>Condition <c>i &lt; 3</c></cell>
          <cell>What happens</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>start</cell><cell>0</cell><cell></cell><cell><c>i</c> is set to 0</cell></row>
        <row><cell>check</cell><cell>0</cell><cell><c>true</c></cell><cell>prints 0, then <c>i++</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell>check</cell><cell>1</cell><cell><c>true</c></cell><cell>prints 1, then <c>i++</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell>check</cell><cell>2</cell><cell><c>true</c></cell><cell>prints 2, then <c>i++</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell>check</cell><cell>3</cell><cell><c>false</c></cell><cell>loop is over, prints <c>"done"</c></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The body ran 3 times. The values of <c>i</c> inside the body were 0, 1, and 2.
      The variable <c>i</c> did reach 3, but only long enough to make the condition <c>false</c>.
      The body never saw it.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here is the same loop so you can run it and compare.
      Change the <c>3</c> to another number and predict the output before you run it again.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="try-flow-1">
      <title>Trace It Yourself</title>
      <program xml:id="ac-flow-1" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
      <code>
for (let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}
console.log("done");
      </code>
    </program>
    </activity>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-why-loops">
    <title>Why the Loop Is Worth It</title>

    <p>
      When a loop is short, writing the lines out by hand might seem just as easy.
      The difference shows up when you need to change something.
      If the body needs a new line, you add it once and every pass gets it.
      If the number of passes changes, you change one number.
      Code that is written out by hand, line by line, is called <term>hard coded</term>,
      and every change has to be made in every copy.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-flow-of-execution-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-flow-order" label="mc-flow-order">
      <statement>
        <p>In what order does a <c>for</c> loop do its work after the start?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>condition, body, update, condition, body, update, ...</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. The condition always comes before the body.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>body, condition, update, body, condition, update, ...</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. In this order, the body would run once even if the condition were <c>false</c> from the beginning.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>update, condition, body, update, condition, body, ...</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. In this order, the loop variable would change before the first pass.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-flow-count-1" label="mc-flow-count-1">
      <statement>
        <p>Which choice lists every value <c>i</c> has inside the loop body, in order?</p>
        <program language="javascript">
          <code>
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {
    console.log(i);
  }
          </code>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>0, 1, 2, 3</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. It starts at 0 and stops when <c>i &lt; 4</c> is <c>false</c>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>1, 2, 3, 4</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. The start sets <c>i</c> to 0, so 0 is the first value.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>0, 1, 2, 3, 4</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. When <c>i</c> is 4, the condition is <c>false</c>, so the body does not run.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>1, 2, 3</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. The start sets <c>i</c> to 0, and the body runs with that value.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-flow-never" label="mc-flow-never">
      <statement>
        <p>How many times will this loop's body run?</p>
        <program language="javascript">
          <code>
  for (let i = 10; i &lt; 5; i++) {
    console.log(i);
  }
          </code>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>0 times</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. <c>10 &lt; 5</c> is <c>false</c> on the very first check, so the body never runs.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>1 time</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. The condition is checked before the body, not after.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>5 times</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. The loop does not run until the condition becomes <c>false</c>. It runs only while the condition is <c>true</c>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>It never stops</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. A loop that never stops needs a condition that stays <c>true</c>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-flow-after" label="mc-flow-after">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the last thing this program prints?</p>
        <program language="javascript">
          <code>
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
    console.log("in the loop");
  }
  console.log("after the loop");
          </code>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>after the loop</c>, once</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. That line is after the closing <c>}</c>, so it runs once, after the loop is over.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>after the loop</c>, three times</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. Only code inside the curly braces repeats.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>in the loop</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. Control flow keeps going after the loop is over.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
